[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"MySQL Read-Write Splitting and Proxy Architecture"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As database workloads scale, scaling read operations becomes essential for maintaining application performance. Read-Write Splitting distributes database traffic by directing write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to a primary master database and read operations (SELECT) to one or more replica read-only instances. This tutorial explores the architecture, implementation patterns, and proxy-based routing for MySQL."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A standard read-write splitting topology consists of a Primary Node, Asynchronous or Semi-Synchronous Replication, and Replica Nodes. The application or an intermediate database proxy must intercept SQL statements and route them intelligently."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Primary Node: Handles all transactional workloads, data modifications, and acts as the source of truth."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Replica Nodes: Synchronize data from the primary and serve analytical or high-volume read queries."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Database Proxy: Sits between the application layer and MySQL instances, handling routing logic transparently."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Proxy-Based Architecture vs Application-Level Routing"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"While routing can be implemented inside application ORMs (like Hibernate or custom connection managers), using a database proxy (such as MySQL Router, ProxySQL, or MaxScale) decouples infrastructure topology from application code."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Proxy-Level: Centralized configuration, connection pooling, automatic failover, and transparent query parsing."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Application-Level: Requires custom data source configuration, complex framework dependencies, and difficult connection scaling."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring ProxySQL for Read-Write Splitting"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"ProxySQL is a high-performance, protocol-aware proxy for MySQL. Below is a step-by-step configuration to set up backend servers and routing rules."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"INSERT INTO mysql_servers (hostgroup_id, port, hostname) VALUES (10, 3306, '192.168.1.10');
INSERT INTO mysql_servers (hostgroup_id, port, hostname) VALUES (20, 3306, '192.168.1.11');
LOAD MYSQL SERVERS TO RUNTIME;
SAVE MYSQL SERVERS TO DISK;"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the configuration above, hostgroup_id 10 represents the primary (writer) node, while hostgroup_id 20 represents the replica (reader) nodes."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Query Routing Rules"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, configure ProxySQL query rules to send SELECT statements to the read hostgroup, while ensuring writes and explicit transactions target the primary."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"INSERT INTO mysql_query_rules (rule_id, active, match_pattern, destination_hostgroup, apply) VALUES (1, 1, '^SELECT.*', 20, 1);
INSERT INTO mysql_query_rules (rule_id, active, match_pattern, destination_hostgroup, apply) VALUES (2, 1, '^SELECT.*FOR UPDATE', 10, 1);
LOAD MYSQL QUERY RULES TO RUNTIME;
SAVE MYSQL QUERY RULES TO DISK;"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Addressing Replication Lag Challenges"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous replication introduces lag. If a user updates their profile and immediately reads it back, they might hit a replica that has not yet processed the binary log event. Solutions include:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Force Read to Primary: Route reads to the primary node immediately after a write operation using session-based stickiness."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GTID-Based Consistency: Leverage Global Transaction Identifiers (GTID) to wait for replicas to catch up before executing a read query."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implementing read-write splitting via a proxy architecture like ProxySQL or MySQL Router significantly scales database read throughput without modifying application source code. Proper handling of replication lag and failover logic ensures high availability and data consistency in production environments."}]
